        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Nazism</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">1919 CE – 1945 CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Thinkers</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a>, whose personal views the movement treated as final on ideology; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-goebbels">Joseph Goebbels</a>, propagandist and anti-capitalist radical; Alfred Rosenberg, who introduced Hitler to the Protocols of the Elders of Zion; Hans F. K. Günther, racial theorist; Carl Schmitt, legal theorist of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuhrerprinzip">Führerprinzip</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-himmler">Heinrich Himmler</a>, head of the SS; Gregor and Otto Strasser, anti-capitalist rivals; earlier influences <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-gottlieb-fichte">Fichte</a>, Gobineau, Houston Stewart Chamberlain, Madison Grant, Johann Plenge and Oswald Spengler</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Key texts</span><span class="info-stat-val">Hitler’s Mein Kampf and Zweites Buch; the party programme of 1920 (the 25-point plan); Goebbels’s Der Nazi-Sozi; Günther’s Rassenkunde des deutschen Volkes (1922); Schmitt’s Staat, Bewegung, Volk (1933); among its sources Chamberlain’s The Foundations of the Nineteenth Century (1899), Grant’s The Passing of the Great Race (1916) and Spengler’s The Decline of the West (1918)</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Core ideas</span><span class="info-stat-val">a racial hierarchy with a supposed Aryan master race; fervent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antisemitism">antisemitism</a>; Lebensraum, or living space, through conquest in the East; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuhrerprinzip">Führerprinzip</a>, absolute obedience to a single leader; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volksgemeinschaft">Volksgemeinschaft</a>, a racial people’s community without class conflict; anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> and rejection of liberal democracy; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-darwinism">social Darwinism</a> and eugenics; a totalitarian state</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Today</span><span class="info-stat-val">Nazism became widely regarded as evil after 1945. Only a few fringe racist groups, usually called neo-Nazis, describe themselves as its followers, and the use of Nazi symbols is illegal in many European countries.</span></div>
