    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Nazism combined German ultranationalism, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antisemitism">antisemitism</a> and pseudo-scientific racial theory with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuhrerprinzip">Führerprinzip</a> and the aim of winning Lebensraum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>, and it took much of its material from movements that existed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> before 1914.</li><li>Hitler was appointed Chancellor in January 1933 by President Hindenburg with the support of conservative nationalists who believed they could control him; the Nazis then used the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag-fire-decree">Reichstag Fire Decree</a> and the Enabling Act to build a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a>.</li><li>Under Nazi rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Romani</a>, political opponents and other people deemed undesirable were marginalised, imprisoned or murdered, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a> millions, including two-thirds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>’s Jewish population, were exterminated.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The word nazi was a colloquial, derogatory word for a backward farmer before the party existed; the party’s labour-movement opponents shortened its name to it in the 1920s, and the Nazis themselves first used it in 1926, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-goebbels">Joseph Goebbels</a>’s Der Nazi-Sozi.</p></aside>
    </section>

